{"date":"2026-11-06","decided_at":"2026-11-06T04:35:11+00:00","hash":"40d891ef98c489772829c01be2530d17dfcbeb09473db9368736789b478f5c70","model":"claude-opus-5-5","prev_hash":"6eb6761aae1b25f62656f426b772a71f8caae3886975054106ca96cb45892e30","reasoning":"Nothing clears the bar this week, and the account stays flat. (1) Oil: crude is pricing a binary outcome, a peace deal or continued attacks on Hormuz shipping. The Nov 3 marks are already $8-10 stale, so entry at the next official mark would come after a large, unknowable gap, and a stop tight enough to keep risk sensible would sit inside the daily noise. That leaves no edge in either direction (sources: tradingeconomics.com/commodity/crude-oil/news/598646; theglobeandmail.com/investing/article-oil-tumbles-as-investors-weigh-end-to-iran-war-after-us-democrats/; unitedagainstnucleariran.com/analysis/iran-shipping-update-november-5-2026). (2) US10Y: a lower oil price argues for a bond rally. Against that, the Fed has a hiking bias, with two dissents for a hike and a strong 55.5 ISM manufacturing reading. The 15bp fall in yields over the past week has already priced part of the oil move (sources: cnbc.com/2026/10/28/fed-interest-rate-decision-october-2026.html; seekingalpha.com/article/4958991). (3) JPY: there are arguments for a long. The US and Japan have intervened jointly before, and cheaper oil helps Japan's terms of trade. But MUFG notes persistent structural outflows and a weak link between rate gaps and the yen, and negative carry against a hiking Fed eats into any payoff. My honest probability is about 52%, which is not worth paying costs for (sources: tradingpedia.com/2026/11/04/yen-climbs-on-intervention-fears-softer-dollar/; tmgm.com MUFG yen note). (4) FR-DE: the spread could tighten further from 150bp or widen sharply again depending on the budget vote. I have no informational edge on that vote (source: pandaequityresearch.substack.com/p/the-spread-is-only-the-first-price). Finally, I used up my search budget before I could check the BoE decision, October payrolls, the BoJ and the ECB. Without those, I can't judge gilts, sterling, the euro, the Bund, the Swiss franc, the Aussie or the Canadian dollar. I will look again next week, once oil and the data have settled.","recorded_at":"2026-11-06T04:36:17+00:00","rule_notes":[],"run_seq":2,"seq":3,"type":"no_trade"}
